  <!-- BUYING GUIDE -->
  <section id="buying-guide" style="padding: 4rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e7eb; background: #f9fafb;">
    <div style="max-width: 1200px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.75rem; font-weight: 800; color: #111827; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">Key Buying Considerations Before You Choose a Hoodie Manufacturer</h2>
      <p style="color: #374151; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 2rem; max-width: 700px;">Most guides list factories. This section tells you what to actually verify before committing to any production order.</p>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 1.5rem;">
        <div style="background: #fff; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.75rem;">
          <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; color: #111827; margin: 0 0 0.75rem;">1. Ask for a GSM Test Report, Not Just a Fabric Description</h3>
          <p style="color: #374151; line-height: 1.75; margin: 0;">Every factory will tell you their hoodie is 400 GSM. Very few will send an independent test report proving it. Request a third-party GSM test certificate for the specific fabric being used in your production. A 20 GSM variance between what was quoted and what was produced can make a premium hoodie feel like a budget product.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="background: #fff; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.75rem;">
          <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; color: #111827; margin: 0 0 0.75rem;">2. Specify Your Zipper Brand in Writing</h3>
          <p style="color: #374151; line-height: 1.75; margin: 0;">YKK zippers are the global standard and worth specifying by name in your tech pack. Generic zippers can break after a few washes, which creates returns and destroys brand trust. Always confirm the zipper brand in your purchase order and request the zipper spec sheet from the factory before production begins.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="background: #fff; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.75rem;">
          <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; color: #111827; margin: 0 0 0.75rem;">3. Test Shrinkage Before Mass Production</h3>
          <p style="color: #374151; line-height: 1.75; margin: 0;">Fleece fabrics can shrink 3 to 8 percent after washing. Always request a pre-production shrinkage test. The factory should wash sample fabric and report the shrinkage percentage before cutting. If they skip this step, you may receive hoodies that are a full size smaller after the first wash.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="background: #fff; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.75rem;">
          <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; color: #111827; margin: 0 0 0.75rem;">4. Calculate Total Landed Cost Before Comparing Quotes</h3>
          <p style="color: #374151; line-height: 1.75; margin: 0;">The FOB price is just the starting point. Add freight, import duties, customs fees, and any local delivery costs to get your true landed cost. A hoodie quoted at $12 FOB from Bangladesh can land at $17 to $20 in the USA depending on shipping method and duty rates. Always compare landed costs, not just FOB prices.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="background: #fff; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.75rem; border-left: 4px solid #ef4444;">
          <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; color: #111827; margin: 0 0 0.75rem;">Red Flags to Walk Away From</h3>
          <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #374151; line-height: 1.8;">
            <li>Cannot provide verifiable certification numbers for claimed credentials</li>
            <li>Refuses to send a fabric GSM test report</li>
            <li>Does not specify zipper brand in their quote</li>
            <li>Pushes back on shrinkage testing requests</li>
            <li>Quotes change significantly after you place the order</li>
            <li>Cannot provide factory photos or a virtual factory tour</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- GSM GUIDE -->
  <section id="gsm-guide" style="padding: 4rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e7eb;">
    <div style="max-width: 1200px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.75rem; font-weight: 800; color: #111827; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">GSM and Fabric Guide for Hoodies</h2>
      <p style="color: #374151; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; max-width: 700px;"><strong>What is GSM in hoodie manufacturing?</strong> GSM stands for grams per square meter. It measures how heavy the fleece fabric is. A higher GSM means a thicker, heavier hoodie. A lower GSM means a lighter, more breathable one.</p>
      <p style="color: #374151; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 2rem; max-width: 700px;">Choosing the wrong GSM is one of the most common and expensive mistakes in hoodie manufacturing. Here is the complete breakdown.</p>
